<%# locals: (rotation:, plan:, identities:) %>
<%#
  AN EVICTION WHOSE BROADCAST ANSWER WE NEVER GOT.

  ── WHY THIS PANEL EXISTS AT ALL ─────────────────────────────────────────

  The row is `submitted` and CLAIMED, and that is correct: the claim is what
  stops a rotation that already landed being sent a second time. On this page
  a second attempt would be authorized by the very keys the first one evicted,
  so it fails `Unauthorized` and reads to the operator like his eviction did
  not work — mid-incident, on the one control he has.

  What was wrong was that `#show` scoped the console to `.pending`, so the row
  simply DISAPPEARED and the planner rendered in its place. The evidence was
  gone and the only remedy left was a Rails console, which resolves an incident
  by deleting the record of it (/tasks/stranded-eviction-has-no-door).

  ── WHAT THIS PANEL MAY AND MAY NOT OFFER ────────────────────────────────

  Exactly one action: Reconcile, which asks the CHAIN what became of this
  signature and acts on the answer. No co-sign, no rebuild, no discard. Each of
  those would either re-send the rotation or file a possible on-chain fact as a
  draft that never happened.

  IT RENDERS BEFORE THE `vault.nil?` REFUSAL, on purpose. Surfacing this row
  needs no chain read — it is a database fact — and the RPC that lost the
  broadcast's answer is the same RPC that feeds this page, so the two failures
  arrive together. A refusal meant to stop a rotation being PLANNED blind must
  not also take away the only way to resolve one already sent.
%>
<div class="rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface-alt p-3 mb-4"
     data-eviction-stranded
     data-eviction-slug="<%= rotation.slug %>">
  <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-danger-ink mb-2">
    Broadcast — the chain's answer was never read back
  </p>

  <p class="text-sm text-secondary mb-3">
    This eviction was sent and the reply was lost. It may have landed. It stays
    claimed and will <strong>not</strong> be re-sent, because a second rotation
    would be authorized by the keys the first one just removed — it would fail
    and read like the eviction had not worked.
  </p>

  <% if plan.present? %>
    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 mb-3">
      <div>
        <p class="label-upper text-3xs text-muted mb-1">Signer set it started from</p>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5">
          <% Array(plan[:current]).each do |address| %>
            <%= render "armed_wallet", address: address, user: identities[address],
                       tone: (Array(plan[:evicted]).include?(address) ? "text-danger-ink line-through" : "text-heading") %>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="label-upper text-3xs text-muted mb-1">Signer set it was writing</p>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5">
          <% Array(plan[:proposed]).each do |address| %>
            <%= render "armed_wallet", address: address, user: identities[address],
                       tone: (Array(plan[:added]).include?(address) ? "text-primary" : "text-heading") %>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <% if rotation.tx_signature.present? %>
    <p class="label-upper text-3xs text-muted mb-1">Transaction</p>
    <p class="font-mono text-2xs break-all text-heading mb-3"><%= rotation.tx_signature %></p>

    <%= button_to "Reconcile against the chain",
                  admin_reconcile_authority_rotation_path(rotation.slug),
                  method: :post, class: "btn btn-primary btn-sm" %>

    <p class="mt-2 text-3xs text-muted">
      Reconcile asks the chain about this exact signature. If it landed, the row
      is left alone and you read the signer set back below — that read, not a
      status flag, is the authoritative answer on this page. If it failed on
      chain, or its blockhash window has lapsed so it can never land, the
      eviction is armed again and can be rebuilt. While it may still land,
      nothing changes and you reconcile again shortly.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <%# LEGACY: claimed with no signature. `#claim_for_broadcast!` cannot produce
        this state any more — it stamps the signature in the same statement that
        takes the claim — but rows claimed by the older code have no handle to
        ask the chain with, and #reconcile would refuse them. Showing the button
        anyway would teach an operator mid-incident that the remedy is broken. %>
    <p class="text-sm text-warning-ink">
      This eviction was claimed by an older version of this console, which did
      not record the transaction's signature — so there is no handle to ask the
      chain with, and it cannot be reconciled here. Read the vault signer set
      below to see what the chain actually holds. If it did not change, find the
      transaction on chain and post its signature through the treasury console's
      confirm step, which verifies it before writing anything.
    </p>
  <% end %>
</div>
